# GPG and encryption

The user's GPG key is on a YubiKey that stays connected, and its PIN is usually cached.

- Run GPG signing, decryption, and encryption yourself, including SOPS re-encryption and edits; when a pinentry dialog appears, let it reach the user.
- Stop and report only when the PIN is rejected, the prompt times out, or the card is missing.
- Never print decrypted values, pass them as arguments, or open decrypted files with the file tools; pipe decrypted output straight into the command that consumes it.
- Leave card administration and key management to the user.
- For a new SOPS setup or secret file, use the card key `{{ .user.gpgKey }}` as the user's recipient, plus a machine's own recipient when it must decrypt without the card. Keep the existing method in a project that already has one.

# Git

- Before the first push, rename a branch whose name does not describe the change, such as a worktree codename or random hex, with `git branch -m`. Follow the project's documented naming rule; otherwise name it `<kind>/short-kebab-slug` with a full-word GitHub Flow kind: `feature/`, `fix/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, or `refactor/`. Never rename onto or push over an existing branch.
- When a commit hook fails because mise does not trust a `mise.toml` in a temporary copy the hook made, retry that commit once with `MISE_TRUSTED_CONFIG_PATHS="$(cd "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}" && pwd -P)"`. Do not run `mise trust` on the copy or skip the hook with `--no-verify`.
- Before each push, find the learnings under the project's Compound Engineering `solutions/` directory that cite a path or name the pushed commits delete, move, or rename. Refresh each with `ce-compound-refresh`, commit each refresh separately on the current branch, then push once.

# Pull and merge requests

- Treat GitLab like GitHub: when a Compound Engineering skill speaks only GitHub, do the same steps with `glab`, and skip only a step with no GitLab counterpart, naming it in your report.
- When you run `gh`, `glab`, or another CLI that reads the host and repository from the current Git checkout in a directory whose remotes do not name the target repository, such as a temporary directory or a clone whose `origin` is a local path, pass the host and repository explicitly. Read them from the target project's own checkout, from its `upstream` remote when that points at a server and otherwise from `origin`, never from a default such as `github.com` or `gitlab.com`. Pass `--repo HOST/OWNER/REPO` to a `gh` subcommand that takes `--repo`, and pass `HOST/OWNER/REPO` as the argument of one that takes the repository as an argument, such as `gh repo view`. Give `glab` the repository's full URL instead, through `--repo` or as the argument, because it can read a bare `HOST/OWNER/REPO` as a project on `gitlab.com`. Prefix any other `gh` command, `gh api` included, with `GH_REPO=HOST/OWNER/REPO` and any other `glab` command, `glab api` included, with `GITLAB_REPO` set to the full URL, for that one command and never exported, and also pass `--hostname HOST` to `gh api`, which ignores the host in `GH_REPO`. In the same way, prefix every `glab` command with `GITLAB_HOST=HOST`, because `glab repo clone` and `glab repo delete` send their requests to `gitlab.com` without it, even with a full URL or `GITLAB_REPO`. Write a branch into an API endpoint yourself, because `{branch}` and `:branch` still come from the current checkout.
- After you open or push to a pull or merge request, babysit it until it looks ready to merge, then leave the merge to the user. Babysit a draft only when asked. On GitLab, call it ready only when `head_pipeline` passed on the current `sha`, `detailed_merge_status` is `mergeable`, and no discussion is unresolved; never rebase.
- Attach the screenshots or recordings a reader needs to see the change. Before uploading, cover secrets, personal data, and internal hosts with opaque boxes, never blur, and strip metadata with `ffmpeg -map_metadata -1`. Upload through `gh --attach` or the GitLab uploads API, never a browser.

# Markdown

- When you write a range in text that renders as Markdown, in any language, write it in prose, not in a code span, with a dash, as in `1-3` or `10:00-12:00`, or an escaped tilde, as in `1\~3`, and never with a bare `~` or `~~`, so tildes never render as strikethrough.
- Treat a tilde that marks an approximation, such as `~5 min`, the same way: write "about 5 min" or `\~5 min` in prose, not in a code span.
- This covers chat replies, pull request and issue bodies and comments, and Markdown documents; put any other tilde, such as a path like `~/.config` or a Git revision like `HEAD~1`, in a code span, and leave a tilde inside a code span or code block as it is.
- In text that does not render as Markdown, such as commit messages, code comments, and plain-text or config files, write a range with a dash or a word, and an approximation with a word, and never with `\~`.

# Korean text

- Korean text that outlasts the conversation is covered text: commit messages, pull and merge request bodies and comments, issue bodies and comments, documents, Korean inside code such as comments, string literals, user-facing messages, logs, and localization resources, and a closing report, summary, or handoff to the user.
- A short conversational chat reply is not covered text.
- Before you write covered text, load the `humanizer`, `grammar-checker`, and `style-guide` skills, once per session.
- Write covered text by those skills' rules, check it with all three before you publish it, and apply every correction you find.
- Apply them silently: do not paste their analysis tables, grades, or change summaries into the text or the conversation.
- These instructions outrank those skills' own procedures: publish only the corrected text, never call `AskUserQuestion` or stop for confirmation on their behalf, and infer the closest document type when it is unclear.
- Where those skills' rules conflict with the Markdown section above, such as a bare tilde in a range, follow the Markdown section.
- Leave text the user asks you to post as written, and verbatim quotes, unchanged.
- When a report also goes through `ce-noslop`, run `ce-noslop` first, then `humanizer`, `grammar-checker`, and `style-guide` in that order.
- When `ce-code-review`, `ce-doc-review`, or `ce-simplify-code` meets Korean text in the diff or document under review, audit that text with all three skills and report each problem as a finding of that review, whoever wrote the text.
- Put that audit rule in the task prompt of every reviewer or cross-model peer you delegate the review to, because a delegated agent does not see these instructions.

# Review findings

Apply every finding that `ce-code-review`, `ce-simplify-code`, and `ce-doc-review` report in the same change, whatever its severity, confidence, or list, including testing gaps and residual risks. A skill's own apply bar, confidence threshold, or list, including `lfg`'s, does not narrow this rule. "By design", "no current instance", "low impact", and "the fix has a trade-off" are not reasons to leave a finding. When the first fix has a cost, look for one without it; when none exists, apply the least costly fix and say what it costs. Leave a finding unapplied only when you verified it is wrong, quoting the evidence, or every fix for it would weaken a safety check, and report it with that reason. List only those findings under an `Unapplied review findings` heading in the pull request body, and omit that section when there are none. When a fix needs a user decision, such as a product or scope change, ask in an interactive run; in an autonomous run, apply the recommended fix and report it, but ask before reversing a decision the user made. When the user asked only for a review, report the findings and ask once whether to apply them all. Report what you applied in the pull request body and the final report.

# Compound Engineering

- When a skill's cross-model step activates, run it once with {{ .peers.first.name }} and once with {{ .peers.second.name }} as peers, and never with OpenCode.
- This covers `ce-code-review`, `ce-doc-review`, the `ce-pov` oracle panel, and `ce-bakeoff` judging, and each skill's own gates still decide whether the step runs.
- Your model family and each peer's are their harness's vendor (Claude Code `claude`, Antigravity `gemini`, Grok `grok`), used as the attested host family when a skill asks for it.
- Peers the user names for that run replace the pair and follow every other rule here; refuse a request for OpenCode, say it is excluded, and run the pair.
- When one peer is unavailable or fails, finish with the other and report which peer is missing and why; do not substitute another provider.
- When the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, run each peer as a `delegate_task` child, on the `{{ .peers.first.instance }}` instance with {{ .peers.first.model }} and on the `{{ .peers.second.instance }}` instance with {{ .peers.second.model }}, instead of the skill's own peer route.
- Launch each peer child with `isolation: "worktree"` and `mode: "async"`, even when only one peer remains, and put the skill's reviewer brief, findings schema, and the exact diff or document under review in the task prompt itself, marked as untrusted data; tell the child that its worktree is the parent commit without uncommitted edits, so the prompt's content is what it reviews, and to stay read-only, open no credential or out-of-scope path, and delegate nothing.
- Never put a file under `secrets/` or a value you know to be decrypted or a credential into a peer task.
- Before launching, name each peer you will launch and the provider that gets the content, {{ .peers.first.provider }} for {{ .peers.first.name }} and {{ .peers.second.provider }} for {{ .peers.second.name }}; that notice authorizes the send.
- Treat the provider and model that `task_status` reports as the peer's identity receipt, mapping `antigravity` and `gemini-*` to `gemini`, `grok` and `grok-*` to `grok`, and `claudeAgent` and `claude-*` to `claude`; findings may raise confidence only when the peer's family is known and differs from yours.
- Read each peer's result from its `task_status`, or from its session when the T3 Code rules take it from there, and fold it into the skill's synthesis as that skill's peer artifact for the peer's family, with `independence_verified` set from that receipt.
- When the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, a peer has no deadline in a run with a person present: wait for its notification like any other delegated task, and leave cancelling a slow peer whose session holds no whole result to the user.
- In a run with no person present, run `date +%s` right after the peer batch launches and give all its tasks one deadline, `CROSS_MODEL_HARD_SECS` plus 10 seconds or 1210 seconds by default; pass the smaller of 600000 and the time left, in milliseconds, as `timeoutMs` on each `t3_thread_wait`, and at the deadline treat a peer still running as the T3 Code rules treat a task whose bound has passed: take a whole result from its session, and otherwise cancel it with `task_cancel` and count it as failed.
- Outside T3 Code, run the skill's own peer route once per peer in turn, naming each peer as the target of its pass; `ce-bakeoff` has no such route, so use its native judge and report the missing peers.
- In `ce-work`, prefer delegating each implementation unit to a worker named below unless the repository's Compound Engineering config sets `work_engine_mode: off`.
- Never delegate an implementation unit to Grok or OpenCode, inside or outside T3 Code.
- In a Grok or OpenCode session, delegate every implementation unit to a worker named below instead of writing it yourself.
- When the config sets `work_engine_mode: off`, a Grok or OpenCode session implements no unit itself: it stops and tells the user that delegation is off.
- In `ce-work`, classify each implementation unit into `quick`, `writing`, `visual-engineering`, `unspecified-low`, `unspecified-high`, or `deep`, splitting independent mechanical or small edits into `quick` or `unspecified-low` units while keeping cohesive hard work whose shared reasoning would suffer if split as one `unspecified-high` or `deep` unit.
- A unit is `quick` when it is a mechanical edit, such as a rename, a pinned-text sync, boilerplate, a config tweak, or a one-file copy fix, even across files; it goes to `claude-haiku-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance with `effort` `medium` as primary and to `gemini-3.8-flash` at thinking level `low` on the `antigravity` instance as fallback.
- A unit is `writing` when it authors or revises documentation, READMEs, guides, or prose beyond a mechanical one-line sync; it goes to `gemini-4-argon` at thinking level `low` on the `antigravity` instance as primary and to `claude-opus-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance with `effort` `low` as fallback.
- A unit is `visual-engineering` when it builds or styles frontend UI, UX, CSS, layouts, animation, or design-system components; it goes to `gemini-4-argon` at thinking level `low` on the `antigravity` instance as primary and to `claude-opus-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance with `effort` `max` as fallback.
- A unit is `deep` when its own design is hard, such as a new interface, data format, or concurrency rule, when it debugs subtle cross-module behavior, or when it carries a security or data-loss risk, even when its edit is mechanical, and `unspecified-high` when it changes several modules across known patterns; both go to `claude-opus-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance as primary, with `effort` `max` for `deep` and `medium` for `unspecified-high`, and to `gemini-4-argon` at thinking level `medium` on the `antigravity` instance as fallback.
- Every other unit is `unspecified-low`, which goes to `claude-sonnet-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance with `effort` `medium` as primary and to `gemini-3.8-flash` at thinking level `high` on the `antigravity` instance as fallback.
- Inside T3 Code, launch each unit on its category's primary worker first, and when that worker is unavailable or its launch fails, give the unit to its category's fallback worker with that worker's own effort rule.
- When the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, launch each unit through `delegate_task` with the worker's instance and model id as `target`, and, for a Claude worker, its `effort` in `target.options`, instead of Compound Engineering's own delegation route.
- When the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, T3 Code does not set a Gemini thinking level yet, so launch every Gemini task on the `antigravity` instance with no `target.options`; the thinking level named beside a Gemini model is the level meant for it.
- Outside T3 Code, Compound Engineering's own route keeps one recipient and one effort for a whole run, so give it only the `unspecified-low` category as ordered candidates, `claude` with `claude-sonnet-5-5` and then `antigravity` with `gemini-3.8-flash`, at that route's default effort, `high`.
- When `ce-code-review` or `ce-doc-review` runs its Antigravity pass outside T3 Code, add `CROSS_MODEL_EFFORT_OVERRIDE=medium` to that pass's start invocation and pass no model override.

# T3 Code

- When the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, such as `delegate_task`, launch every subagent through `delegate_task`, including the scouts, verifiers, reviewers, and workers a skill dispatches, and never use your harness's own subagent or multi-agent tools, even where T3 Code's own guidance prefers them for same-provider work.
- When the session lists no T3 Code delegation tools, use your harness's own subagent tool.
- The delegation rules that follow apply only when the session lists those tools.
- When no skill or rule names a model for a subagent, launch it on your own provider instance with the model from that instance's list in `orchestrator_capabilities` that you judge fits the tier the skill names, or with your own model when the skill names no tier or no listed model fits it, and when `orchestrator_capabilities` marks a default `effort` for that model, set `effort` in `target.options` to it, even for your own model.
- In a Grok or OpenCode session, a subagent that edits files follows the implementation-unit rules of the Compound Engineering section.
- A delegated subagent sees none of your conversation and none of the tool limits its native agent type had, so put everything it needs in its task prompt, including that it stays read-only when it only searches, reads, or reviews.
- Tell each delegated subagent to launch no subagents of its own, so delegation stays one level deep.
- Launch every delegated task, cross-model peers and implementation units included, with `runtimeMode: "full-access"`, so it never pauses on an approval nobody sees.
- When `delegate_task` rejects that launch with an error that names the runtime mode, because your thread runs below Full Access or the target provider does not offer that mode, relaunch the task once without `runtimeMode`, so it inherits your thread's mode, under a new `clientRequestId`; that rejection is not a failed or unavailable launch under any rule in these instructions, including the launch-failure retry below, a category's fallback worker, and a failed cross-model peer.
- When a `delegate_task` launch fails, relaunch it once with the same `clientRequestId`; when that fails too, take the next fallback these instructions name, such as a category's fallback worker, and when none is left, do the work yourself instead of using your harness's own subagent tool, unless these instructions forbid it, as they do for a cross-model peer and for an implementation unit in a Grok or OpenCode session.
- Before you edit a file that a still-running task also edits, in this checkout or in a worktree you will merge back, wait until that task has finished and its edits are merged.
- Launch every delegated task that does not need another task's result in the same response, each with `delegate_task` in its default `async` mode, instead of launching one, waiting for it, and then launching the next.
- Never use `mode: "wait"`, even for a single task, because it holds your turn until the task finishes.
- Never run two tasks that edit the same files in one checkout at the same time: from a clean checkout, launch each with `isolation: "worktree"`, tell each to commit its edits and report its branch, and merge those branches back one at a time; otherwise launch them one after another.
- Tell tasks that share one checkout not to commit, stage, or format files beyond their own, and commit their results yourself.
- Give each delegated task its own `clientRequestId`, and reuse it when you retry that launch.
- Launch a task that needs an earlier task's result only after you have read that result.
- In a run with a person present, after launching, do the work that does not need the delegated results, and when only those results remain, end your turn so the user can talk to you while the tasks run.
- When you end your turn while tasks still run, name them, and when one of them runs without `runtimeMode: "full-access"`, also say that such a task can pause on an approval, which shows "Subagent needs approval" in its thread and waits for the user, because T3 Code sends no notification for it.
- Each notification T3 Code delivers, whether it starts your turn or arrives with a user message, names the tasks that reached a terminal state or have a question; read each finished task's result with one `task_status` call.
- While another task the waiting step needs is still running, end your turn again without cancelling it, and continue that step once every result it needs has arrived through a notification or from the task's session.
- When a notification says a delegated task has a question, read it with `t3_pending_request_read`, then answer it with `t3_pending_request_respond` or ask the user, and end your turn again.
- When the user writes while delegated tasks are running, send any answer they give to a pending question to its task with `t3_pending_request_respond`, answer the rest, keep the tasks and the waiting step, and end your turn again unless they redirect the work.
- On every turn you get while a task the waiting step needs has not reached a terminal state and you have not taken its result, whether a notification, a user message, a background wait, or a `t3_thread_wait` timeout started it, read its `task_status` once, then read its session with `t3_thread_read` on its `childThreadId` in the `activity` view, paging with `afterPosition` until no `nextPosition` remains, and read its latest assistant message by `itemId`, repeating with `textOffset` set to `nextTextOffset` until `nextTextOffset` is null, because a task, such as a Grok cross-model peer, can write its result and never finish.
- When that `task_status` is terminal, use its result as usual; when it shows a pending question or `hasPendingChildRuns` true, keep waiting.
- Otherwise, when the latest assistant message is finished, holds the whole result the task prompt asked for, and nothing in the session has changed since your previous check of it, use the message as the task's result, stop the task with `task_cancel`, and say that you took its result from the session.
- When `task_cancel` leaves that task running, keep the result, tell the user once that it still runs so they can stop it, and count its result as taken.
- In a run with a person present, never wait for a delegated task with `t3_thread_wait`; in any run, never call `task_status` in a loop, and never start a sleep, timer, or background command whose only purpose is to wake yourself later. A delay a test must reproduce, a retry interval, a wait the user asked for, and a wait that blocks on the task's own run stay allowed.
- Ending your turn to wait for a delegated task is allowed even where a skill says not to end the turn or to fail when no blocking wait exists, such as `lfg`'s rule not to end the turn before `<promise>DONE</promise>` or `ce-code-review`'s reviewer collection; when the results arrive, continue the same run from the step that waited.
- For such a skill, a completion notification and one `task_status` read, or a result taken from the task's session, are the task's terminal outcome, and a task the skill bounds by time counts as failed when you resume after that bound has passed and its session holds no whole result.
- In a run with no person present, such as a headless, scheduled, or pipeline run, wait inside the turn instead: once every task is launched, call `t3_thread_wait` on each task's `childThreadId` with its `childRunId` as `runId` and, as `timeoutMs`, the smaller of 600000 and the time left before any bound on that task, then read each result with one `task_status` call; when no time is left before that bound, skip the wait and apply the bound rule below.
- When that wait returns `timedOut: true`, answer a pending question with `t3_pending_request_respond` from the task and your instructions, check the task as above, and wait again with the same `runId` unless you took its result or its bound has passed.
- When a skill bounds a delegated task by time and the bound passes while it still runs, check it as above, and when its latest finished assistant message holds the whole result, take it as above even if the session changed since your previous check; otherwise cancel it with `task_cancel` and count it as failed.
- When `task_status` shows `workState` `working` or `hasPendingChildRuns` true, call `t3_thread_wait` on that thread again without `runId` and with `timeoutMs` as above; when `childRunId` is null, or the task shows `waiting_for_children` with `hasPendingChildRuns` false, there is no run to block on, so check it as above, take a finished message that holds the whole result, count the task failed only when the bound has already passed at that check and the session holds no whole result, and otherwise end your turn with no timer and no further check, and name the tasks when a person is present.
- When the session lists T3 Code's pull request tools, such as `watch_pull_request`, use them to watch a request you babysit instead of your own background wait.
- When the session lists T3 Code's preview tools, such as `preview_open`, show any local HTML file you open for the user in a T3 Code browser tab instead of running `xdg-open` or `open`: run `serve-html <file>`, open a new tab with `preview_open` and `reuseExistingTab` set to false so the user's own tab stays as it is, then call `preview_navigate` with that tab's `tabId` and the line `serve-html` printed as `target`. Pass `--root <dir>` to `serve-html` when the page loads sibling files from a report directory. When the user asks to see a page again, or says its tab no longer loads, run `serve-html` again instead of reusing an earlier target, and navigate the same tab to the new target. A run with no person present, such as a headless or pipeline run, still opens nothing. When the session lists no T3 Code preview tools, or `serve-html`, `preview_open`, or `preview_navigate` fails, keep the skill's own way of opening the file and print the file's absolute path.
